[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I reproduced connectivity between the two adjacent PCs and found the network path healthy. Both hosts are in the same subnet (172.18.51.0/24): PC15=172.18.51.15 and PC16=172.18.51.16 with eth0 up/LOWER_UP. Bidirectional pings succeed with 0% loss and low RTTs (~0.5–1.0 ms). Throughput tests (iperf) in both directions sustain ~150–160 Mbps, confirming no filtering or blocking at L2/L3.

On the access switch (switch10), both user ports are up and forwarding in the same bridge domain:
- PC15 on switch10 eth1 and PC16 on switch10 eth2 are members of br0, state “forwarding,” link up. No STP blocking or port shutdown is present.
- Interfaces report link detected, full-duplex.

Mechanism: If a switch port were disabled or isolated, L2/L3 tests would fail (no ARP, no ICMP, no TCP). Instead, ICMP and TCP flows traverse normally, proving the switch fabric and host NICs are functioning.

Localization: Access segment on switch10 (eth1 for PC15 and eth2 for PC16) and both host NICs.

Root cause: The reported outage is not present; PC16’s switch port is already active and passing traffic.

Corrective fix: No changes required on the network. Please re-test file sharing using the correct IPs (172.18.51.15 ↔ 172.18.51.16). If application access still fails, verify the file-sharing service is running and allowed by the host firewall on both PCs; the underlying network connectivity is confirmed good.
=== END ===